\details{
The PTRM score measures local terrain realism on a continuous scale. Higher
values indicate more realistic terrain patterns according to the perception
model of Rajasekaran et al. (2022). The focal version applies this metric
locally to map spatial variation in terrain complexity.

\strong{Computational Complexity:}
\itemize{
\item Geomorphons: O(N × 8R) where R = search radius
\item Focal PTRM: O(N × W) where W ≈ π × radius²
\item Total: O(N × (8R + W))
}

For a 1000×1000 DEM with radius=5, search=7:
\itemize{
\item Expected runtime: 3-6 seconds on modern multi-core system
\item Memory: ~20 MB
}
}
\section{Algorithm}{

Uses a two-pass approach for efficiency:
\enumerate{
\item \strong{Calculate geomorphons globally} (if \code{mode = "dem"})
\itemize{
\item Applies the full geomorphon classification algorithm once
\item Produces classification raster with classes 1-10
}
\item \strong{Apply focal PTRM calculation} to each pixel's neighborhood
\itemize{
\item For each interior pixel, extracts geomorphon classes within circular window
\item Calculates proportions of each geomorphon form
\item Applies PTRM formula using empirical coefficients
\item Returns spatially-varying PTRM scores
}
}

This two-pass approach is much more efficient than recalculating geomorphons
for each window (~38x speedup for typical parameters).
}

\section{Window Type}{

Uses a circular window based on Euclidean distance. For an integer
\code{radius = r}, includes all cells where \code{sqrt(dx^2 + dy^2) <= r}.
}

\section{Border Handling}{

Cells within \code{radius} distance of raster edges are set to NA, as their
neighborhoods are incomplete. This prevents edge artifacts in the output.
}

\section{Memory Requirements}{

For small/medium rasters, uses the fast C++ implementation requiring
approximately 4 bytes per input cell plus 8 bytes per output cell (about
12 bytes per cell) in RAM. For large rasters that exceed half of the
available memory, automatically falls back to \code{terra::focal()}, which is
slower but memory-efficient.
}

